The Security Council,

Reaffirming its full commitment to the peace process in the Republic of Colombia and its unwavering support for the 2016 Final Agreement for Ending the Conflict and Building a Stable and Lasting Peace (the Final Agreement),

Recalling all its relevant resolutions and presidential statements on the situation in Colombia, in particular resolutions 2366 (2017), 2574 (2021), 2673 (2023), and 2798 (2025),

Reaffirming Colombia’s sovereign ownership of the implementation of the Final Agreement, and commending the continued commitment of the Government of Colombia and the signatories to advancing peace, reconciliation, and transitional justice,

Welcoming the work of the Special Jurisdiction for Peace (JEP) in pursuing accountability, truth, and justice for victims, and noting the critical role of the Verification Mission in verifying compliance with and implementation of the restorative sentences of the JEP,

Acknowledging the essential importance of the comprehensive implementation of the Final Agreement, including provisions on comprehensive rural reform, the security and reintegration of former combatants, the ethnic chapter, and the gender provisions,

Expressing continued concern over threats, attacks, and violence targeting former combatants, social leaders, human rights defenders, women, and vulnerable and conflict-affected communities, including Indigenous and Afro-Colombian communities, and emphasizing the necessity of strengthening the integrated presence and protection mechanisms of the State across conflict-affected areas,

Recognizing the vital contributions of the United Nations Verification Mission in Colombia and the Special Representative of the Secretary-General in supporting the Colombian people and institutions in building a stable and enduring peace,

1. Decides to extend the mandate of the United Nations Verification Mission in Colombia until 31 October 2027;

2. Reaffirms that the Verification Mission shall continue to carry out its verification and monitoring tasks in accordance with its mandate as set out in resolutions 2366 (2017), 2574 (2021), 2673 (2023), and 2798 (2025);

3. Calls upon the Government of Colombia and all relevant stakeholders to accelerate the comprehensive implementation of the Final Agreement, with particular attention to the security of ex-combatants and communities, comprehensive rural reform, and the implementation of restorative sentences issued by the Special Jurisdiction for Peace;

4. Requests the Secretary-General to continue to report to the Security Council on the implementation of the Verification Mission’s mandate every 90 days;

5. Expresses its willingness to continue working with the Government of Colombia to support the full realization of the Final Agreement.

The Security Council,

Reaffirming its full commitment to the peace process in the Republic of Colombia and its unwavering support for the 2016 Final Agreement for Ending the Conflict and Building a Stable and Lasting Peace (the Final Agreement),

Recalling all its relevant resolutions and presidential statements on the situation in Colombia, in particular resolutions 2366 (2017), 2574 (2021), 2673 (2023), and 2798 (2025),

Reaffirming that the peace process must remain Colombian-owned and Colombian-led, reiterating Colombia’s sovereign ownership of the implementation of the Final Agreement, and commending the continued commitment of the Government of Colombia and the signatories to advancing peace, reconciliation, and transitional justice,

Welcoming the work of the Special Jurisdiction for Peace (JEP) in pursuing accountability, truth, and justice for victims, and noting the critical role of the Verification Mission in verifying compliance with and implementation of the restorative sentences of the JEP,

Acknowledging the essential importance of the comprehensive implementation of the Final Agreement, including provisions on comprehensive rural reform, the security and reintegration of former combatants, the ethnic chapter, and the gender provisions, emphasizing that advancing rural reform and promoting development are essential to eliminating the root causes underlying the conflict, and stressing the need to guarantee the full, equal, and meaningful participation of women and civil society in all aspects of the peace process,

Expressing continued concern over threats, attacks, and violence targeting former combatants, social leaders, human rights defenders, women, and vulnerable and conflict-affected communities, including Indigenous and Afro-Colombian communities, and emphasizing the necessity of strengthening the integrated presence and protection mechanisms of the State across conflict-affected areas,

Recognizing the vital contributions of the United Nations Verification Mission in Colombia and the Special Representative of the Secretary-General in supporting the Colombian people and institutions in building a stable and enduring peace,

1. Decides to extend the mandate of the United Nations Verification Mission in Colombia until 31 October 2027;

2. Reaffirms that the Verification Mission shall continue to carry out its verification and monitoring tasks in accordance with its mandate as set out in resolutions 2366 (2017), 2574 (2021), 2673 (2023), and 2798 (2025);

3. Requests the Secretary-General to ensure coordination and efficiencies, including by ensuring that other activities in country are carried out by the most appropriate UN system entity;

4. Calls upon the Government of Colombia and all relevant stakeholders to accelerate the comprehensive implementation of the Final Agreement, with particular attention to the security of ex-combatants and communities, the protection of social leaders and human rights defenders, comprehensive rural reform, the ethnic chapter, gender provisions, and the implementation of restorative sentences issued by the Special Jurisdiction for Peace;

5. Requests the Secretary-General to continue to report to the Security Council on the implementation of the Verification Mission’s mandate every 90 days, and further requests the Secretary-General to include in these reports an assessment of mission efficiencies and recommendations regarding an eventual transition and exit strategy, including objective benchmarks;

6. Expresses its willingness to continue working with the Government of Colombia to support the full realization of the Final Agreement.

